--TEST--
a Core\IO\Metadata answers about the moment its `stat` was made and never about the file afterwards — a value taken before an append still reports the old size while a fresh one reports the new, and the second modification time is never before the first — spec § 14
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\IO\Metadata::size, Core\IO\Metadata::modifiedAt, Core\IO\Metadata::isFile, Core\IO\Metadata::isDir
// What `stat` buys is one syscall for four questions; what it costs is that
// the answers are frozen. This is the bound on both sides: the same member of
// the same value, asked before and after the file changed under it, and a
// second value taken afterwards that does see the change.
Core\IO::write("note.txt", "hello world\n");

var $before = Core\IO::stat("note.txt");
int $was = $before->size() as int;
echo "before: ", $was as string, "\n";

Core\IO::append("note.txt", "and more\n");

// The held value has not moved. A live view would print 21 here, and a member
// that re-stat'ed on every call would look identical to a caller who only ever
// asked once -- which is why the question is asked twice of one value.
int $held = $before->size() as int;
echo "held: ", $held as string, "\n";

var $after = Core\IO::stat("note.txt");
int $now = $after->size() as int;
echo "after: ", $now as string, "\n";

if ($now > $was) {
    echo "a fresh stat sees the append\n";
}

// The modification time moves the same way, and only forwards. `compareTo`
// answers -1, 0 or 1, so "not before" is the assertion a filesystem whose
// timestamps are coarse can still satisfy -- a write inside one tick of the
// clock leaves the two equal, and that is not a failure.
if ($after->modifiedAt()->compareTo($before->modifiedAt()) >= 0) {
    echo "the later stat is not the earlier one\n";
}

// `Core\IO::modifiedAt` reaches the file rather than the snapshot, so it
// agrees with the value taken after the append and not with the one before.
if (Core\IO::modifiedAt("note.txt")->compareTo($after->modifiedAt()) == 0) {
    echo "the member agrees with the fresh snapshot\n";
}

// The kind of thing at the path did not change, and neither did the answer.
// The partition is over the snapshot exactly as it is over the path: a regular
// file answers `true` to one of these and `false` to the other, and appending
// to it cannot move it from one side to the other.
if ($before->isFile() && $after->isFile()) {
    echo "still a file on both sides\n";
}
if ($before->isDir() || $after->isDir()) {
    echo "one snapshot called it a directory\n";
} else {
    echo "neither snapshot called it a directory\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
before: 12
held: 12
after: 21
a fresh stat sees the append
the later stat is not the earlier one
the member agrees with the fresh snapshot
still a file on both sides
neither snapshot called it a directory
